1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,880
My name is James Rosing.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:08,080
I'm a surgeon in Newport Beach, California, and for more than 14 years, I've been getting letters like this one.

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:10,260
Everything you will see was built this week.

4
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:14,620
My record system, appeal engine, and coverage index are pre-existing and not shown.

5
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,240
So this letter is fake.

6
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:18,380
However, the reason is real.

7
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:20,380
Not medically necessary.

8
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:23,840
A payer software writes a denial in about a second.

9
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:29,240
Appealing it takes a physician hours, so most denials never get appealed, and the patient ends up paying.

10
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:37,950
Some say AI cancels out in this situation because both sides can buy the same model, but nobody can buy the chart.

11
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:43,150
A denial is a claim about the payer's policy, and the payer holds the policy.

12
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:48,590
An appeal is a claim about the medical record, and only the physician holds the record.

13
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:51,270
The imbalance isn't in intelligence.

14
00:00:51,770 --> 00:00:52,650
It's in evidence.

15
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:55,810
This project gives the advantage to the side with the evidence.

16
00:00:56,310 --> 00:01:02,230
If the chart passes a deterministic gate, an appeal draft gets written for the physician to sign.

17
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:04,870
Here's one of 50 synthetic cases.

18
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:06,090
No real patients.

19
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:14,850
A 47-year-old woman was prescribed radiation planning after a lumpectomy, and her Medicare Advantage plan denied it.

20
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:25,010
Muse Spark 1.3 first asks CHART, my pre-existing coverage index, when the plan's rules last changed, and then opens the entries it needs.

21
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:29,150
Every sentence it writes quotes one entry and names it.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,700
As each sentence comes in, a deterministic gate checks that the quote really appears in the entry, and that every number, date, and code matches the source.

23
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:44,020
Here, every sentence was verified, in 12 seconds for 2 cents.

24
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:48,830
Now change one number in the verified sentence, and the gate refuses it.

25
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:51,750
The model writes the draft, but the record decides.

26
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:59,370
The payer can run the same gate on my appeal, so the dispute gets settled on the record, not on whose bot writes better.

27
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:03,980
When the chart can't support an argument, the model says so.

28
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:10,920
Here, Muse Wonder read all 22 entries, looking for a sepsis criterion, found nothing, and declined.

29
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:20,670
Across 50 cases, three Meta models, Muse Spark, Muse Wonder, and Muse Spark Open, the open-weights model, running on Baseten, and 9 runs.

30
00:02:21,170 --> 00:02:25,070
Agreement with the answer key ranged from 44 to 50 out of 50.

31
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:27,970
Not one sentence contained a fabricated quote.

32
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:30,430
Spark's runs cost under a dollar each.

33
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:35,970
I injected more than twelve thousand fabrications to attack the gate, and it caught every hard one.

34
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:44,400
This is what the physician sees, the appeal, the payer's reason answered directly, every sentence footnoted to the entry it quotes.

35
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:48,700
It stays a draft until the physician signs it.

36
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:54,750
The gate is on npm as litmus-gate, and either side of the table can use it.

37
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:56,710
Thank you so much for this opportunity.

